% ============================================================================
% cloud-signal — for cirrus and stratus clouds, the INDIVIDUAL weather
% signal(s) an already-cited NWS sentence lists for each, decoded one-per-row
% (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `cloud-type.adj` (`cloud_type(cloud,
% weather_indication)`, each of three common cloud types and the single
% combined weather indication its presence signals). Two of that table's own
% three quoted NWS sentences each list MORE than one signal, joined by an
% exhaustive "or" list -- a fact the single `weather_indication` atom folded
% into one compound label, with no room to decode each listed item as its
% own queryable fact:
%
%   cirrus (two alternatives joined by "or", in the parent table's own
%   `source` field):
%     "They often are the first sign of an approaching warm front or
%      upper-level jet streak."
%
%   stratus (three alternatives, an "or"/"or" chain, quoted in the parent
%   table's own header):
%     "Stratus clouds are uniform and flat, producing a gray layer of cloud
%      cover which may be precipitation-free or may cause periods of light
%      precipitation or drizzle."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? cloud_signal(stratus, $Signal)
%       % precipitation_free, light_precipitation, AND drizzle (three answers)
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `cloud_signal(cloud, signal)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the
% engine returns each signal WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on a cloud whose own cited span names only ONE signal
% with no listed alternative. `cumulonimbus` is deliberately NOT a row here:
% its own already-quoted span ("a mature, deep cumulonimbus cloud, i.e., a
% thunderstorm producing heavy rain") names exactly one signal -- no
% enumerated "or"/list of alternatives to decode -- so this sibling is
% HONESTLY NARROWER than its parent, grounding exactly the two cloud types
% whose cited spans list more than one signal and abstaining on the third,
% or any other word, rather than inventing one. The query also runs
% BACKWARD -- bind a signal and recall which cloud type indicates it:
%
%     ? cloud_signal($Cloud, upper_level_jet_streak)
%       % cirrus
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two NWS sentences
% `cloud-type.adj`'s own `source` field and header already quote in full for
% its cirrus and stratus rows -- no new WebFetch, only a different schema
% decoding each listed signal as its own row instead of folding the whole
% clause into one compound `weather_indication` atom. An ADJ `table` carries
% ONE provenance envelope, so `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the
% cirrus span (matching `cloud-type.adj`'s own convention of quoting each
% row's own source sentence in prose); the stratus row's own span is listed
% above for independent auditability. `trust authoritative` -- the same tier
% `cloud-type.adj` already carries (NWS Louisville forecast office, a
% primary U.S. government science-education source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table cloud_signal {
    columns cloud, signal

    row (cirrus, approaching_warm_front)
    row (cirrus, upper_level_jet_streak)
    row (stratus, precipitation_free)
    row (stratus, light_precipitation)
    row (stratus, drizzle)

    source "They often are the first sign of an approaching warm front or upper-level jet streak."
    locator "https://www.weather.gov/lmk/cloud_classification"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Stratus clouds are uniform and flat, producing a gray layer of cloud cover which may be precipitation-free or may cause periods of light precipitation or drizzle." locator "https://www.weather.gov/lmk/cloud_classification"
}
